    "evidence_text": "This suggests that a model's ability to capture generalizable structure is highly sensitive.\n\nMoreover, this sensitivity may not be apparent when evaluating it on standard test sets.\n\nWe refer to this notion as linguistic generalization .\n\nMany problems in NLP are treated as sequence to sequence tasks with solutions built on seq2seq-attention based models.\n\nHowever, learning structure is not a sufficient condition to achieving linguistic generalization.\n\nOne may hope, given the right hyperparameter\n\nconfiguration and regularization, that a model converges to a solution that captures the reusable structure without overfitting too much on the training set.\n\nIn this work, we look at the feasibility of training and tuning seq2seq-attention models towards a solution that generalizes in this linguistic sense.\n\nIn particular, we train models on a symbol replacement task with a well defined generalizable structure.\n\nGeneralization in a Symbol Rewriting Task\tReal\n\nAs done in previous work  ,  , we ease analysis by looking at a simple formal task.\n\nAs an example, let $X = \\lbrace A,B\\rbrace $ , $Y_A = \\lbrace\n\nTraining is stopped once accuracy on the validation set either decreases or remains unchanged.\n\ncharacteristic inputs to decide the epoch to stop at.\n\nAll other hyperparameters were also decided using this validation set.\n\nFurther training details are listed in Table 1 .\n\nin a single sample.\n\nThe second set tests the model's ability to generalize to repeated symbols in the input (Repeat).\n\nTable 2 provides further information on the 4 different test sets.\n\nHowever, the deviation on the other two sets is much larger, ranging from\n\nThe highest average performance across the non standard test sets for any seed was 79.52%.\n\nRelated Work\t  provides one of the earliest works investigating the ability of RNNs to capture properties of language necessary for generalization.\n\nFurther work explored how RNNs can learn context free\n\nlanguages  ,  as well some context sensitive languages  .\n\nhas questioned the systematicity with which these models learn.\n\nThe same work also makes similar observations about the difficulty of generalizing to longer length strings.\n\nOur experiments indicate that the final model's ability to generalize is highly dependent on the random seed.\n\ngeneralization.\n\nAcknowledgements\tThis work is supported in part by the National Science Foundation under Grant IIS-1617969.",
